if you were turned off by Demon Souls  and Dark Souls don't let it discourage  you from playing blood-borne my own  relationship with the souls games is  fractured because I found them far too  hard I love the idea of video games not  holding your hand of taking away all  directional markers and quest logs and  forcing you to explore and learn your  way through the world by trial and error  developer from software trusts its  players to put the time in but  unfortunately for me I felt that souls  learning curve was a little too steep  however I feel that blood-borne is the  more accessible successor to the souls  games  make no mistake blood-borne pulls no  punches right from the start you're  thrown into the world of yharnam with  much fanfare and none of it positive the  world and its inhabitants are all out to  get you and the game immediately opens  on your first steps in preparing for  combat against them the most obvious  element that makes blood-borne dare we  say easier than its predecessors is that  if you die the game doesn't knock off a  fraction of the total health available  to you in Dark Souls 2 death meant you  lost 10% of your health bar until you  used a special item to regain it in  blood-borne you'll keep your health  points after death this is especially  helpful as enemies progressively require  more than a few blows and quite a bit of  stamina to kill I know I'm grateful to  keep my hard-earned health points when I  have to repeatedly face down the same  boss conveniently all upgrades and items  you need can be purchased in your hub  world hunter stream hunters dream is an  accurate name for it as it's seriously a  dream both in terms of what it offers  you and how pretty it looks the hub  world is more centralized in previous  souls games so you don't have to run all  over yarn I'm looking for the nitty  gritty that you need blood warns level  design encourages you to explore between  it's lengthy checkpoints by removing any  waypoints and making Direction hints  subtle by not giving a means to track  where you are and where you need to go  other than your own memory blood-borne  tricks you into moving forward an  environment design relies less on gotcha  moments to help you understand what  you're up against then previous souls  games there are no moments set up to  kill you just to kill you no cheap traps  there's less reliance on throwing unseen  pitfalls in your way instead they are  obstacles for you to observe learn from  and surmount blood-borne setup isn't  cruel it's a never-ending challenge that  feels  even handed bloodborne also makes sure  you always have the tools you need to  survive whether or not you use them to  your advantage is entirely up to you for  example you always have access to health  restoring blood vials both in hunters  dream and as a common enemy drop both  blood vials and Quicksilver bullets are  dropped by many enemies you kill  ensuring that you'll never be without in  the souls games refilling Estus flasks  required you to either die or return to  the hub world enemies didn't drop health  items but in blood-borne if you run out  of blood or bullets chances are a nearby  enemy will also have something you need  this rewards pushing forward / playing  it safe all of this boils down to a  world that might not be fun all the time  but it's definitely satisfying to master  blood-borne is a smidge easier than  other souls games and that it keeps your  health steady and your inventory  well-stocked but that doesn't mean the  enemies you'll face are less difficult  you'll fight to earn your status as a  master hunter make no mistake but  blood-borne is at its heart a more  accessible introduction to the souls  games for newcomers
 

